A documentation of what I learned building PlayStack for the Sheryians Coding School Mini Hackathon
Live demo: https://sheryians-mini-hackathon-playstack.vercel.app/ GitHub repo: https://github.com/Blaezy/sheryians-mini-hackathon-playstack
Press enter or click to view image in full size

Introduction
Before this hackathon, I’d never touched Redux Toolkit. I’d built a few React projects using just useState, and honestly, that had always been enough. So when this challenge landed with zero lectures and just a doc link and a YouTube video, my first reaction was "how am I supposed to learn a whole state management library on my own before class even starts?"
But that’s kind of the point of the challenge, isn’t it. So I picked a project idea I actually cared about — a game library tracker called PlayStack, where I could log what I want to play, what I’m currently playing, and what I’ve finished, complete with ratings and tags. Then I just started building, hit walls, read docs, and figured it out.
This is a walkthrough of what I actually learned, using the real code from my project instead of textbook definitions.
What is Redux, and why Redux Toolkit
Redux is a pattern for managing state in a way that’s predictable and centralized. Instead of every component holding its own bits of data, you have one single store that holds everything, and components read from it and request changes to it — they never change it directly themselves.
The problem with plain Redux (before Toolkit) is that it needed a lot of boilerplate. You had to write action types as string constants, write action creators as separate functions, and write reducers using switch statements while manually spreading objects to avoid mutating state directly. It worked, but it was verbose and error-prone.
Redux Toolkit (RTK) is Redux’s official “batteries included” version. It gives you createSlice, which bundles your state, your actions, and your reducers into one clean object — no separate action type constants, no switch statements, and (this genuinely surprised me) you're allowed to write code that looks like direct mutation.
Core concepts, explained through my actual slice
Here’s my gameSlice.js, which is the heart of the whole app.
import { createSlice } from "@reduxjs/toolkit";export const gameSlice = createSlice({
name: "games",
initialState: {
games: JSON.parse(localStorage.getItem("games")) || [],
},
reducers: {
addGame: (state, action) => {
state.games.push(action.payload);
localStorage.setItem("games", JSON.stringify(state.games));
},
updateGame: (state, action) => {
const game = state.games.find((game) => game.id === action.payload.id);
if (game) {
Object.assign(game, action.payload.updates);
}
localStorage.setItem("games", JSON.stringify(state.games));
},
removeGame: (state, action) => {
state.games = state.games.filter((game) => game.id !== action.payload);
localStorage.setItem("games", JSON.stringify(state.games));
},
removeFilteredGames: (state, action) => {
state.games = state.games.filter((game) => game.status !== action.payload);
localStorage.setItem("games", JSON.stringify(state.games));
},
},
});
Exporting the actions and reducer:
export const { addGame, updateGame, removeGame, removeFilteredGames } = gameSlice.actions;
export default gameSlice.reducer;The Store
The store is the single object holding all my app’s state. I don’t create it directly inside gameSlice.js — createSlice just describes one piece of the store. The actual store gets assembled in store.js:
import { configureStore } from "@reduxjs/toolkit";
import searchReducer from "./features/searchSlice.js";
import gameReducer from "./features/gameSlice.js";
export const store = configureStore({
reducer: {
search: searchReducer,
game: gameReducer,
},
});This is where my two slices — game and search — get combined into one store. Anywhere in my app, state.game.games gives me the array of games, and state.search.query gives me the current search text. Splitting things into separate slices like this kept my "games list" logic completely separate from my "search UI" logic, even though both live in the same global store.
A Slice
A slice is a self-contained chunk of state plus the logic to change it. My gameSlice doesn't know or care that a searchSlice exists — it only manages one thing: the array of games. This separation is honestly one of the things that clicked for me the most — instead of one giant reducer trying to handle everything, each slice owns its own little piece of the puzzle.
Reducers
A reducer is a function that describes how state changes in response to an action. Notice something interesting in my addGame reducer:
addGame: (state, action) => {
state.games.push(action.payload);
localStorage.setItem("games", JSON.stringify(state.games));
},I’m calling .push() directly on state.games. In plain JavaScript, mutating state directly like this is a big no-no in Redux — normally you'd have to do something like state.games = [...state.games, action.payload] to avoid actually mutating the original array.
But Redux Toolkit wraps every reducer with a library called Immer. Immer lets you write code that looks like direct mutation, but behind the scenes, it’s tracking your changes and producing a proper new immutable state object. This was one of my favorite things to learn — it removes almost all the “don’t forget to spread this object” boilerplate that makes plain Redux tedious.
Actions
An action is just a plain object describing “what happened.” When I call addGame(newGameObject), RTK's createSlice automatically generates an action creator for me — I never had to write { type: "games/addGame", payload: newGameObject } by hand. That object gets built and returned automatically just from naming the reducer addGame.
useDispatch and useSelector
These are the two hooks that connect my React components to the Redux store.
useDispatch gives me a function to send actions to the store. Here's how I use it when a user adds a new game, from GameDetailsStep.jsx:
const dispatch = useDispatch();
const handleAdd = () => {
if (alreadyPresent(game)) return;
dispatch(
addGame({
id: game.id,
name: game.name,
image: game.image,
year: game.year,
status,
rating: status === "played" ? rating : null,
hours: status === "played" ? Number(hours) || null : null,
tags: status === "played" ? tags : [],
notes: "",
})
);
onClose();
};dispatch(addGame({...})) sends the action to the store. The gameSlice's addGame reducer catches it, pushes the new game into the array, and saves the updated array to localStorage — all in one step.
useSelector is the opposite direction — it's how a component reads from the store. Here's MainPage.jsx pulling the games list and filtering it:
const games = useSelector((state) => state.game.games);
const wantFilteredGames = games.filter((g) => g.status === "want");
const playingFilteredGames = games.filter((g) => g.status === "playing");
const playedFilteredGames = games.filter((g) => g.status === "played");I don’t store three separate arrays for “want,” “playing,” and “played” in Redux. I only store one flat array, and every time a component needs a filtered view, it derives that view on the fly using .filter(). This was a deliberate design decision — if I kept moving games between three separate arrays, I'd risk bugs where a game exists in two places at once, or disappears entirely during a move. With one array and a status field, moving a game between sections is just changing one property.
Data Flow — how it actually moves through my app
Here’s the full journey of adding a game, step by step:

• User opens the “Add Game” modal and types into the search bar

• That keystroke dispatches setQuery into my searchSlice

• A debounce hook waits 700ms after typing stops, then calls the RAWG API

• Results come back and get dispatched into searchSlice via setResults

• User clicks a game from the results, which moves them to the details step

• User picks a status tab (Want / Playing / Played) — if Played, extra fields (rating, hours, tags) appear

• User clicks “Add Game” — this calls dispatch(addGame({...}))

• The gameSlice reducer pushes the new object into state.games and saves it to localStorage

• Because MainPage (and every other page) is subscribed to state.game.games via useSelector, React automatically re-renders those components with the updated list — I never manually tell any component "hey, refresh yourself"
That last point was the biggest “oh, that’s the whole point” moment for me. I never manually sync data between components. Redux handles that automatically — any component reading from the store re-renders when the store changes, full stop.
Editing works almost identically, just using a different reducer. Here’s EditGameDetails.jsx:
const handleSave = () => {
dispatch(
updateGame({
id: game.id,
updates: {
status,
rating: status === "played" ? rating : null,
hours: status === "played" ? Number(hours) || null : null,
tags: status === "played" ? tags : [],
},
})
);
onClose();
};Instead of pushing a whole new object, updateGame finds the existing game by id and merges in only the changed fields:
updateGame: (state, action) => {
const game = state.games.find((game) => game.id === action.payload.id);
if (game) {
Object.assign(game, action.payload.updates);
}
localStorage.setItem("games", JSON.stringify(state.games));
},Object.assign only overwrites the keys I actually pass in — everything else on that game object stays untouched.
Folder structure
src/
api/gameApi.js -- RAWG API calls
components/AddGame.jsx -- modal: search -> pick game -> details
components/SearchGameStep.jsx -- debounced search input + results
components/GameDetailsStep.jsx -- status tabs + fields for adding
components/EditGameDetails.jsx -- same fields, but for editing
components/GameCard.jsx -- card for Want/Playing games
components/GameColumn.jsx -- maps games to GameCard
components/PlayedGameCard.jsx -- wider card with rating/hours/tags
components/PlayedGameColumn.jsx -- maps games to PlayedGameCard
components/Navbar.jsx
hook/useDebounce.js
layout/MainLayout.jsx -- Navbar, Outlet, and modal state
pages/MainPage.jsx -- the 3-column board
pages/WantPage.jsx
pages/PlayingPage.jsx
pages/PlayedPage.jsx
redux/features/gameSlice.js -- games CRUD + localStorage
redux/features/searchSlice.js -- search query/results/loading
redux/store.js
routes/AppRoutes.jsxSplitting GameCard/GameColumn from PlayedGameCard/PlayedGameColumn was a decision I made once I realized "Played" games needed a completely different layout (rating, hours, tags) compared to "Want to Play" or "Playing" games, which only need a cover image and title.
Important functions I relied on

• createSlice — bundles state + reducers + auto-generated actions into one object

• configureStore — combines all slices into the single app-wide store

• useSelector — reads a piece of state, re-renders the component when that piece changes

• useDispatch — returns the dispatch function used to send actions

• .filter() — used constantly to derive per-status views from one flat array, instead of storing three separate arrays

• Object.assign() — used in updateGame to merge partial updates into an existing object without wiping out untouched fields
Real-world use cases
Redux Toolkit makes the most sense when several unrelated parts of an app need to read or change the same data. In my case, the games list needs to be visible and editable from four different pages (MainPage, WantPage, PlayingPage, PlayedPage) plus the Navbar's Add button — without Redux, I'd be passing that array and its setter function through props across completely unrelated route components, which isn't really possible cleanly with React Router's page structure.
The same pattern shows up in real production apps — shopping cart data shared between a product page, a cart icon in the navbar, and a checkout page; or user authentication state needed by a profile page, a settings page, and a protected-route guard, all at once.
Challen
